  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.5,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_rating",
      "components": ["D2"],
      "pins": ["D2.1", "D2.2"],
      "nets": ["+24V input rail (node after F2 / left of T7)", "GND"],
      "observed": "On the POWER TRAIN sheet the transient-stabilizer group contains D6 = 28V and D2 = 12V, both wired from the 24 V input node (immediately after fuse F2, left of the reverse-polarity MOSFET T7) down to GND. D2 is therefore a 12 V clamp sitting directly across the 24 V rail.",
      "expected": "The specification lists the input TVS as D2 — SMBJ28A, a 28 V clamp ('Input surge/overvoltage protection on the 24 V rail ... value (\"28V\", DO-214AA TVS) matches a 24 V-rail clamp'). Any TVS/zener placed across the 24 V rail must have a standoff/breakdown well above 24 V; a 12 V device must not be tied across it.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Sheet 1, POWER TRAIN: 'Transient voltage stabilizer' group shows D6 28V and D2 12V, both top ends on the +24V input node (after F2, before T7) and both bottom ends on GND."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "D2 — SMBJ28A TVS | Input surge/overvoltage protection on the 24 V rail | BOM footprint/value (\"28V,\" DO-214AA TVS) matches a 24 V-rail clamp"}
      ],
      "impact": "A 12 V breakdown device across a 24 V rail conducts continuously, clamping the rail to ~12 V and sinking large current. This would overheat D2 and blow the input fuse F2 (or trip the supply), so the board would not power up; the 24 V rail could never reach its nominal voltage.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic's reference designators are reshuffled relative to the spec (a different build), but on the input rail a 12 V clamp (D2) is wired across the 24 V node alongside the 28 V TVS (D6), which contradicts the specified 28 V-only input protection and would short the 24 V rail and blow the input fuse."
}

